<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n47540" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">tăta</span></b> , ae, m.
		Gr. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τέττα;</span> Sanscr.
			tātas, = papa. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b> A name by which young children, speaking imperfectly, call their
			father, as with us, <i>dad</i>, <i>daddy</i>, Varr. ap. <i><span class="en">Non. 81, 5</span></i>;
					<i><span class="en">Inscr. Orell. 2813 sq.</span></i>;
				<i><span class="en">4943</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>A bringer up</i>, <i>rearer</i>,
			analog. to mamma, <i><span class="en">Mart. 1, 101, 1</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
